"""M2A adapter — Feng et al., arXiv:2602.07624 (Feb 2026).

Paper: *M2A: Multimodal Memory Agent with Dual-Layer Hybrid Memory for
Long-Term Personalized Interactions.*
Original repo (MIT): github.com/Little-Fridge/M2A
Original setting: long-term personalized chat with multimodal context
(text + images), evaluated on an enhanced LoCoMo (10 conversations,
~621 turns, 214 injected images).

================================================================
Why this baseline is a clean fit
================================================================
M2A is the closest published baseline to "drop in any VLM and run" —
it is model-agnostic at the LLM layer (configurable via env var) and at
the embedding layer (configurable via env var). The original paper
already benchmarked it on three different backbones (GPT-4o-mini,
Qwen3-VL-8B, GLM-4.6V-Flash), so our 1-open + 3-closed grid maps to it
without protocol changes.

================================================================
Architecture (paper §3)
================================================================
Two layers:

  - **RawMessageStore**: append-only, chronological log of all dialogue
    turns + their attached images. No abstraction, no retrieval.
  - **SemanticMemoryStore**: refined observations extracted by a
    MemoryManager VLM. Each semantic entry contains:
        - associated images (refs)
        - auto-generated captions
        - dense text embeddings
        - sparse BM25 representations
        - cross-modal visual embeddings (siglip2-base-patch16-384)

At query time both layers are consulted; an agentic update mechanism
decides what to write into the semantic layer.

================================================================
Public Python surface (paper repo, MIT)
================================================================
    cfg = M2AConfig.from_file("config.toml")    # or env-var driven
    wrap = M2AEvaluationWrapper(cfg)
    wrap.start_conversation(conv_info)
    for turn in dialogue:
        wrap.chat(turn)
    answer = wrap.question(text, image)

We map our `encode/retrieve/oracle_inject` Protocol onto these three.

================================================================
What we adapt and what we don't
================================================================
Faithful:
  * Dual-layer storage semantics (Raw + Semantic) by deferring to their
    `M2AEvaluationWrapper`.
  * Their default text + multimodal embedders (siglip2 + text embed).
  * Their LLM-backbone-agnosticism: switch via `M2A_LLM_MODEL`.

Adapted:
  * No conversation flow per se — each DMV-Bench session maps to a
    fresh `start_conversation`; each anchor encode is one `chat({...})`
    message; each retrieval query is one `question(text, None)`.
  * We don't pipe ground-truth user-id history; M2A's personalization
    edge over text-only RAG should still show in the dual-layer
    storage benefit alone.

Adaptation gap:
  * Personalization-style temporal carryover (their headline strength)
    is partially neutralized because DMV-Bench resets the memory bank
    at task boundaries by design (see `multisession_design.md` §F.4).
    We expect M2A to win on PD-long (within-task drift) but not on
    cross-task carryover, which we don't test anyway.

If the cloned repo is missing or its import path differs from what we
target below, the adapter falls back to a `_LocalDualLayerMemory`
spirit-of-M2A reimplementation built on our own VLM clients and
embedding cache. This is intentionally inferior to running their
actual code (no siglip2 in the fallback path); the docstring lists
the differences honestly so the paper can footnote which mode was
used per run.
================================================================
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import os
import re                              # used by _bm25_scores tokenizer
from dataclasses import dataclass, field
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Any, Dict, List, Optional

from dualmem.baselines.external._common import (
    ExternalBaselineUnavailable,
    ensure_repo_on_path,
    token_overlap,
)
from dualmem.types import Trial


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local fallback: spirit-of-M2A dual-layer memory built on our infra.
# Used when the upstream repo isn't cloned / its API differs.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
@dataclass
class _RawEntry:
    slug: str
    image_path: str
    text: str
    turn_idx: int = 0


@dataclass
class _SemanticEntry:
    slug: str
    image_path: str
    caption: str = ""           # auto-generated by VLM
    semantic_text: str = ""     # refined observation
    # Siglip2 cross-modal image embedding — restored in v2 of this adapter
    # (paper §3.2). When `None`, retrieval degrades to dense-text only.
    siglip_image_emb: object = None     # np.ndarray | None


class _LocalDualLayerMemory:
    """Spirit-of-M2A dual-layer memory used when the upstream repo
    isn't available. Restored cross-modal hybrid retrieval via
    `google/siglip2-base-patch16-384` (HF, ~600 MB, CPU-runnable) — this
    is the paper's main retrieval contribution; without it the local
    fallback would be algorithmically equivalent to Caption. The dense
    text channel is still SBERT-cosine; BM25 sparse channel and the
    full MemoryManager loop remain dropped (§7 in adaptation doc)."""

    # 3-way fusion weights (dense_text + sparse_BM25 + siglip2_cross_modal),
    # restoring the paper's full trimodal retrieval (paper §3.2).
    _TEXT_W = 0.35
    _BM25_W = 0.25
    _SIGLIP_W = 0.40

    def __init__(self, vlm_backend: str = "qwen-vl-7b"):
        self.raw: List[_RawEntry] = []
        self.semantic: Dict[str, _SemanticEntry] = {}
        self._vlm = None
        self._vlm_backend = vlm_backend
        self._turn = 0
        self._siglip = None       # (model, processor); lazy
        self._siglip_available = True

    def _ensure_vlm(self):
        if self._vlm is None:
            from dualmem.vlm import make_vlm
            self._vlm = make_vlm(self._vlm_backend)

    def _ensure_siglip(self):
        """Lazy-load siglip2 image+text encoders. Uses CUDA if available
        (10-100x faster than CPU on the H200 tenancy); falls back cleanly
        to CPU on the login node. On any failure, the adapter degrades to
        text-only mode (a clean graceful degrade)."""
        if self._siglip is not None or not self._siglip_available:
            return
        try:
            from transformers import AutoModel, AutoProcessor
            import torch
            mid = "google/siglip2-base-patch16-384"
            proc = AutoProcessor.from_pretrained(mid)
            model = AutoModel.from_pretrained(mid).eval()
            device = "cuda" if torch.cuda.is_available() else "cpu"
            model = model.to(device)
            self._siglip = (model, proc, torch, device)
        except Exception:
            self._siglip_available = False
            self._siglip = None

    def _siglip_image(self, image_path: str):
        self._ensure_siglip()
        if not self._siglip_available:
            return None
        model, proc, torch, device = self._siglip
        try:
            from PIL import Image
            img = Image.open(image_path).convert("RGB")
            with torch.no_grad():
                inp = proc(images=[img], return_tensors="pt").to(device)
                feats = model.get_image_features(**inp)
                feats = feats / feats.norm(dim=-1, keepdim=True)
                return feats[0].cpu().numpy()
        except Exception:
            return None

    def _siglip_text(self, text: str):
        self._ensure_siglip()
        if not self._siglip_available:
            return None
        model, proc, torch, device = self._siglip
        try:
            with torch.no_grad():
                inp = proc(text=[text], padding="max_length",
                           return_tensors="pt", truncation=True).to(device)
                feats = model.get_text_features(**inp)
                feats = feats / feats.norm(dim=-1, keepdim=True)
                return feats[0].cpu().numpy()
        except Exception:
            return None

    def reset(self):
        self.raw = []
        self.semantic = {}
        self._turn = 0

    def chat(self, slug: str, image_path: str, text: str):
        self._turn += 1
        self.raw.append(_RawEntry(slug=slug, image_path=image_path, text=text,
                                  turn_idx=self._turn))
        if slug not in self.semantic:
            self._ensure_vlm()
            try:
                cap = self._vlm.generate_freeform(
                    system_prompt="You write concise visual captions.",
                    user_text=(
                        "Caption this product in one sentence; focus on "
                        "attributes a memory module could retrieve by."
                    ),
                    primary_image=image_path,
                    max_tokens=80,
                )
            except Exception as e:
                cap = f"<vlm-error: {e}>"
            # Cross-modal image embedding (paper's siglip2 channel).
            siglip_emb = self._siglip_image(image_path)
            self.semantic[slug] = _SemanticEntry(
                slug=slug, image_path=image_path,
                caption=cap, semantic_text=f"{text} {cap}",
                siglip_image_emb=siglip_emb,
            )

    @staticmethod
    def _bm25_scores(query: str, docs: dict, k1: float = 1.5, b: float = 0.75) -> dict:
        """Minimal Okapi-BM25 — restores M2A's sparse retrieval channel
        without an extra dependency. `docs` is slug -> document text."""
        import math
        from collections import Counter
        def toks(s: str):
            return [t for t in re.split(r"\W+", s.lower()) if t]
        q_toks = toks(query)
        doc_toks = {s: toks(t) for s, t in docs.items()}
        if not doc_toks:
            return {}
        dls = {s: len(ts) for s, ts in doc_toks.items()}
        avgdl = (sum(dls.values()) / len(dls)) or 1.0
        N = len(doc_toks)
        # document frequency
        df: Counter = Counter()
        for ts in doc_toks.values():
            for t in set(ts):
                df[t] += 1
        scores = {}
        for s, ts in doc_toks.items():
            tf = Counter(ts)
            score = 0.0
            for q in q_toks:
                if q not in tf or df[q] == 0:
                    continue
                idf = math.log(1 + (N - df[q] + 0.5) / (df[q] + 0.5))
                denom = tf[q] + k1 * (1 - b + b * dls[s] / avgdl)
                score += idf * (tf[q] * (k1 + 1)) / denom
            scores[s] = score
        return scores

    def _combined_scores(self, text: str) -> dict:
        """Trimodal scoring dict[slug -> score]. Used by both `question`
        (top-1) and `question_topk` (ranked top-k)."""
        if not self.semantic:
            return {}
        text_scores = {
            slug: float(token_overlap(text, e.semantic_text))
            for slug, e in self.semantic.items()
        }
        bm25_scores = self._bm25_scores(
            text, {slug: e.semantic_text for slug, e in self.semantic.items()})
        q_emb = self._siglip_text(text)
        siglip_scores = {}
        if q_emb is not None:
            import numpy as np
            for slug, e in self.semantic.items():
                ie = e.siglip_image_emb
                siglip_scores[slug] = (float(np.dot(q_emb, ie))
                                       if ie is not None else 0.0)

        def _norm(d: dict) -> dict:
            if not d:
                return d
            mx = max((abs(v) for v in d.values()), default=1.0) or 1.0
            return {k: v / mx for k, v in d.items()}

        tn, bn = _norm(text_scores), _norm(bm25_scores)
        sn = _norm(siglip_scores) if siglip_scores else {}
        slugs = list(self.semantic)
        if sn:
            return {s: self._TEXT_W * tn.get(s, 0)
                       + self._BM25_W * bn.get(s, 0)
                       + self._SIGLIP_W * sn.get(s, 0) for s in slugs}
        wT = self._TEXT_W / (self._TEXT_W + self._BM25_W)
        wB = self._BM25_W / (self._TEXT_W + self._BM25_W)
        return {s: wT * tn.get(s, 0) + wB * bn.get(s, 0) for s in slugs}

    def question(self, text: str) -> Optional[_SemanticEntry]:
        """Trimodal retrieval (paper §3.2): dense token-overlap ⊕ sparse
        BM25 ⊕ siglip2 cross-modal cosine, min-max normalized, weighted-summed.
        Returns top-1 SemanticEntry."""
        combined = self._combined_scores(text)
        if not combined:
            return None
        best = max(combined, key=combined.get)
        return self.semantic[best]

    def question_topk(self, text: str, k: int = 5) -> list:
        """Trimodal top-k ranked slugs — bypasses the top-1 + insertion-order
        padding that `_AdapterRetriever` would otherwise apply."""
        combined = self._combined_scores(text)
        if not combined:
            return []
        ranked = sorted(combined.items(), key=lambda x: -x[1])
        return [slug for slug, _ in ranked[:k]]


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Public adapter
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
class M2AAdapter:
    """Adapter for M2A's dual-layer hybrid memory.

    Two execution modes, picked automatically:
      - **upstream**: `M2AEvaluationWrapper` from the cloned repo.
        Requires `scripts/setup_external_baselines.sh m2a` to have run
        and env vars `M2A_LLM_MODEL` / `M2A_*_EMBEDDING_MODEL` set.
      - **local fallback**: `_LocalDualLayerMemory`. Always available.

    Implements `dualmem.baselines.Baseline` Protocol.
    """

    name = "M2A"

    def __init__(
        self,
        prefer_upstream: bool = True,
        config_path: Optional[str] = None,
        vlm_backend: str = "qwen-vl-7b",
        repo_name: str = "M2A",
    ):
        self.prefer_upstream = prefer_upstream
        self.config_path = config_path
        self.vlm_backend = vlm_backend
        self.repo_name = repo_name

        self._wrap = None                   # set if upstream mode succeeds
        self._local: Optional[_LocalDualLayerMemory] = None
        self._mode: Optional[str] = None    # "upstream" | "local"
        self._conv_started = False

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    def _ensure_upstream(self):
        """Try the upstream repo. Returns True on success."""
        try:
            ensure_repo_on_path(self.repo_name)
            from m2a.eval_wrapper import M2AEvaluationWrapper  # type: ignore
            from m2a.config import M2AConfig                   # type: ignore
        except Exception as e:
            raise ExternalBaselineUnavailable(
                f"M2A upstream unavailable: {e}. The adapter expects "
                f"external/repos/M2A/m2a/eval_wrapper.py to expose "
                f"`M2AEvaluationWrapper`. If the repo layout differs, "
                f"patch the import here."
            )
        cfg = (
            M2AConfig.from_file(self.config_path)
            if self.config_path else M2AConfig.from_env()  # honors M2A_LLM_MODEL etc.
        )
        self._wrap = M2AEvaluationWrapper(cfg)
        self._mode = "upstream"

    def _ensure_local(self):
        if self._local is None:
            self._local = _LocalDualLayerMemory(vlm_backend=self.vlm_backend)
        self._mode = "local"

    def _ensure_ready(self):
        if self._mode is not None:
            return
        if self.prefer_upstream:
            try:
                self._ensure_upstream()
                return
            except ExternalBaselineUnavailable:
                pass  # fall through to local
        self._ensure_local()

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Baseline Protocol
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    def reset(self):
        if self._mode == "upstream" and self._wrap is not None:
            # Upstream has no documented reset; re-instantiating the
            # wrapper is the safe path. Re-read the same config.
            self._wrap = None
            self._mode = None
            self._conv_started = False
        elif self._mode == "local" and self._local is not None:
            self._local.reset()
            self._conv_started = False
        # Lazy: leave fully-cold state if mode never picked.

    def encode(self, trial: Trial):
        self._ensure_ready()

        if not self._conv_started:
            self._start_conversation()
            self._conv_started = True

        # Upstream signature is `chat(dialogue)` where dialogue is a
        # message dict — schema is repo-defined and not documented in
        # the README. We pass the documented field names and let M2A's
        # MemoryManager decide what to extract. If their schema differs,
        # this is the single line to patch.
        if self._mode == "upstream":
            try:
                self._wrap.chat({
                    "role": "user",
                    "content": trial.encode_text or f"viewing {trial.anchor_slug}",
                    "image": trial.anchor_path,    # file path; their docs imply path-or-PIL
                    "slug": trial.anchor_slug,
                })
            except Exception:
                # If their wrapper rejects our message shape, demote to local.
                self._ensure_local()
                self._local.chat(trial.anchor_slug, trial.anchor_path,
                                 trial.encode_text)
        else:
            self._local.chat(trial.anchor_slug, trial.anchor_path,
                             trial.encode_text)

    def retrieve(self, query_text: str) -> Optional[Trial]:
        self._ensure_ready()
        if self._mode == "upstream":
            try:
                # Their `question(text, image)` returns the agent's full
                # answer text, not a memory entry. We approximate retrieval
                # by asking for the slug; the runner uses anchor_slug for
                # url-match scoring so this is the field we care about.
                answer = self._wrap.question(
                    text=f"Which previously-seen product matches: {query_text!r}? "
                         f"Reply with only its slug.",
                    image=None,
                )
                # Treat the answer as a slug guess.
                return self._stub_trial_for_slug(answer.strip().lower())
            except Exception:
                self._ensure_local()
        # local
        sem = self._local.question(query_text)
        if sem is None:
            return None
        return self._stub_trial_for_semantic(sem)

    def retrieve_topk(self, query_text: str, k: int = 5) -> list:
        """Trimodal top-k ranked slugs from the local dual-layer memory
        (`_AdapterRetriever` prefers this over `retrieve`+padding so the
        agent sees k truly-ranked candidates, not top-1 + junk)."""
        self._ensure_ready()
        if self._mode == "local" and self._local is not None:
            return self._local.question_topk(query_text, k)
        return []

    def oracle_inject(self, trial: Trial) -> dict:
        self._ensure_ready()
        if self._mode == "upstream":
            # Upstream has no oracle path. We approximate by asking it
            # to summarize what it knows about `trial.anchor_slug`.
            try:
                summary = self._wrap.question(
                    text=f"Summarize what you remember about slug={trial.anchor_slug}.",
                    image=trial.anchor_path,
                )
                return {
                    "text": (
                        "Memory (M2A dual-layer; upstream):\n"
                        f"  slug={trial.anchor_slug}\n"
                        f"  recall_summary: {summary}"
                    ),
                    "images": [trial.anchor_path],
                }
            except Exception:
                self._ensure_local()
        # local
        sem = (self._local.semantic.get(trial.anchor_slug)
               if self._local is not None else None)
        if sem is None:
            return {
                "text": f"Memory (M2A; local fallback): no entry for {trial.anchor_slug}",
                "images": [trial.anchor_path],
            }
        raw_for_slug = [r for r in self._local.raw if r.slug == trial.anchor_slug]
        ep = ", ".join(f"t{r.turn_idx}: {r.text!r}" for r in raw_for_slug[:3]) or "none"
        return {
            "text": (
                "Memory (M2A dual-layer; local fallback):\n"
                f"  semantic.caption: {sem.caption}\n"
                f"  semantic.text:    {sem.semantic_text}\n"
                f"  raw.recent:       {ep}\n"
            ),
            "images": [sem.image_path],
        }

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Inspection
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    def dump_memory(self) -> str:
        """Human-readable snapshot of M2A's two storage layers.

        Upstream mode wraps an opaque M2AEvaluationWrapper, so the dump
        only reports the mode + a "talk to upstream" note. Local-fallback
        mode prints the full RawMessageStore + SemanticMemoryStore.
        """
        lines = [f"=== M2A memory (mode={self._mode or 'cold'}) ==="]
        if self._mode == "upstream":
            lines.append("  (upstream M2AEvaluationWrapper is opaque; ")
            lines.append("   inspect via wrap.question('what do you remember?'))")
            return "\n".join(lines)
        if self._local is None:
            lines.append("  (no memory yet — encode at least once)")
            return "\n".join(lines)
        # Local fallback: full dump.
        lines.append(f"-- RawMessageStore ({len(self._local.raw)} entries, append-only)")
        for r in self._local.raw:
            lines.append(f"  t{r.turn_idx}: slug={r.slug!r}  text={r.text!r}  img={r.image_path}")
        lines.append(f"-- SemanticMemoryStore ({len(self._local.semantic)} entries)")
        for i, (slug, e) in enumerate(self._local.semantic.items()):
            lines.append(f"  [{i}] slug={slug!r}")
            lines.append(f"        caption:       {e.caption}")
            lines.append(f"        semantic_text: {e.semantic_text}")
            lines.append(f"        image:         {e.image_path}")
        return "\n".join(lines)

    def _start_conversation(self):
        if self._mode == "upstream":
            try:
                self._wrap.start_conversation({"session_id": "dmv_bench"})
            except Exception:
                self._ensure_local()
        # local doesn't need explicit start.

    def _stub_trial_for_slug(self, slug: str) -> Optional[Trial]:
        """Build a minimal Trial from a slug guess.

        Upstream mode can return arbitrary text (it's an LLM answer);
        in that case we can't actually recover a stored entry. We
        return a stub Trial whose anchor_slug equals the guess, which
        the runner uses for url-match scoring. If `_local` happens to
        be populated (because we degraded mid-run), prefer its path.
        """
        from dualmem.types import Trial as _T, TaskCell
        slug = slug.strip().split()[0] if slug else ""
        anchor_path = ""
        if self._local is not None and slug in self._local.semantic:
            anchor_path = self._local.semantic[slug].image_path
        dummy = TaskCell(
            category="external", variant="m2a", grain_tier=1, leakage_level=0,
            mechanism="descriptive", ceiling="full_pipeline", seed=0,
        )
        return _T(
            cell=dummy, anchor_path=anchor_path, anchor_slug=slug,
            candidate_paths=[], candidate_slugs=[],
            correct_index=-1, encode_text="", recall_text="",
        )

    def _stub_trial_for_semantic(self, sem: _SemanticEntry) -> Trial:
        from dualmem.types import Trial as _T, TaskCell
        dummy = TaskCell(
            category="external", variant="m2a", grain_tier=1, leakage_level=0,
            mechanism="descriptive", ceiling="full_pipeline", seed=0,
        )
        return _T(
            cell=dummy, anchor_path=sem.image_path, anchor_slug=sem.slug,
            candidate_paths=[], candidate_slugs=[],
            correct_index=-1, encode_text="", recall_text="",
        )
